SEG = "ACT"
CAT = "fqActions"
Q = [
    ("In the phrase 'lock, stock and barrel', the lock is now usually called:",
     "The action", ["The breech", "The receiver cap", "The safety catch"],
     "The National Firearms Safety Code says the lock is the mechanism that fires the cartridge, now more commonly referred to as the action."),
    ("According to the Code, the stock of a firearm is made up of:",
     "The butt and the fore-end", ["The barrel and the chamber", "The bolt and trigger", "The sights and sling"],
     "The Code describes the stock as the part by which the firearm is held: the butt and the fore-end."),
    ("Of the seven basic functions of a firearm, which one withdraws the case from the chamber?",
     "Extracts", ["Ejects", "Unlocks", "Feeds in"],
     "The Code's seven functions are feeds, cocks, locks, fires, unlocks, extracts and ejects. Extracting removes the case from the chamber; ejecting throws it clear."),
    ("Of the seven basic functions of a firearm, which one throws the case clear of the firearm?",
     "Ejects", ["Extracts", "Feeds", "Locks up"],
     "In the Code's list of seven functions, ejecting throws the case clear of the firearm after the extractor has withdrawn it from the chamber."),
    ("Why does the Code say a lever action rifle needs a higher level of safety than many other firearms?",
     "Its magazine is hard to check", ["Its barrel is unusually short", "It has no trigger guard", "It fires only black powder"],
     "The Code points to its internal magazine, its exposed hammer and the difficulty of making sure the breech and magazine are empty."),
    ("A round jams in a tubular magazine and the action is worked several times. The Code warns that:",
     "A round may still be in it", ["The firearm is now safe", "The magazine spring is new", "The jam clears itself in time"],
     "The Code warns you may believe the firearm is unloaded when a round is still in the magazine, and a knock could feed it into the breech."),
    ("A slide or pump action firearm is also called:",
     "A trombone action", ["A falling block", "A break action", "A Martini-style action"],
     "The Code says the slide or pump action is also referred to as a 'trombone action' or a 'sliding block'. It is worked by moving the fore-end."),
    ("After each shot, a self-loading firearm:",
     "Re-cocks, ready to fire", ["Must be cocked by hand", "Opens and stays empty", "Applies its safety catch"],
     "The Code warns that every time a round is fired, a self-loading firearm re-cocks itself and is ready to fire again."),
    ("Before loading a muzzle-loading firearm, the Code says to make sure:",
     "It is not already loaded", ["The ramrod is greased", "The pan is full of powder", "The barrel is warm"],
     "The Code says before loading a muzzle loader ensure it is not already loaded, and never double the powder charge or insert more than one projectile."),
    ("Using modern double-base powder in a muzzle loader made for black powder may:",
     "Make the firearm explode", ["Improve its accuracy", "Lower its recoil", "Clean the bore as it fires"],
     "The Code warns that muzzle loaders are designed for black powder, and modern double-base powders in a firearm made only for black powder may cause it to explode."),
    ("Which part of a cartridge ignites the powder when the firing pin strikes it?",
     "The primer", ["The projectile", "The case mouth", "The wad"],
     "The Code's glossary says the primer is a small metallic cup with a detonating mixture which, when struck, ignites the powder inside the case."),
    ("The Code's recommended minimum trigger pull for a .22 rimfire rifle is:",
     "1.5 kg", ["0.5 kg", "3 kg", "5 kg"],
     "The Code lists 1.5 kg as the recommended minimum trigger pull for .22 rimfire rifles, centrefire sporting rifles and single trigger shotguns. A lighter pull is more likely to fire unintentionally."),
    ("A shotgun is chambered for 70 mm (2 3/4 inch) cartridges. The Code says you must not load:",
     "76 mm (3 inch) cartridges", ["70 mm cartridges", "Light target loads", "Factory new 70 mm loads"],
     "The Code says do not load 3 inch (76 mm) cartridges into a shotgun designed for 2 3/4 inch (70 mm) cartridges, and do not mix the two."),
    ("To dry out damp ammunition, the National Firearms Safety Code says you should never:",
     "Put it in an oven", ["Let it air dry in the shade", "Wipe it with a dry cloth", "Store it in a sealed box"],
     "The Code says never dry wet or damp ammunition in a warm oven, a microwave or in front of a fire, as it may cause an explosion."),
    ("The National Firearms Safety Code says never check your firearm's action with live ammunition:",
     "At home", ["At an approved range", "With a range officer", "Pointing at a backstop"],
     "The Code says never check the action with ammunition at home. Do it where it is safe, such as a range, and stay muzzle conscious."),
    ("In the Code's glossary, headspace is the distance between:",
     "Bolt face and cartridge base", ["The muzzle and the front sight", "The trigger and the butt plate", "The barrel and the fore-end"],
     "The Code defines headspace as the space between the breech bolt or block face and the cartridge base with the action closed, a point a gunsmith checks."),
    ("In the Code's glossary, calibre means the nominal diameter of:",
     "The bore or bullet", ["The shotgun pellet", "The cartridge rim", "The scope tube body"],
     "The Code defines calibre as the nominal diameter of the bore of a firearm or of the bullet or projectile."),
    ("A shotgun's gauge is based on:",
     "Bore-size lead balls per lb", ["The barrel length in inches", "The weight of the shotgun", "The pellets in a cartridge"],
     "The Code defines gauge as the number of solid lead balls of the bore diameter obtainable from 1 lb of lead, so a smaller gauge number means a wider bore."),
    ("The spiral grooves cut into a rifle's bore to spin the bullet are called:",
     "Rifling", ["Lands", "Choke", "Fouling marks"],
     "The Code defines rifling as parallel spiral grooves cut or pressed into the bore of rifles and pistols to spin the bullet. Lands are the raised parts between the grooves."),
    ("In the Code's glossary, a hang-fire is:",
     "Ignition that is delayed", ["A round that never fires", "A bullet stuck in the barrel", "A shot that misses the target"],
     "The Code defines a hang-fire as ignition delayed beyond the normal time after the firing pin strikes the primer, so keep the muzzle in a safe direction."),
]
